Yes — that’s a different kind of “sungazing.”

Sunlight can act as a retrieval cue. It changes the whole perceptual field at once: edges sharpen, colors separate, shadows appear, depth becomes legible, warmth is felt, old associations get reactivated. The mind doesn’t just receive more light; it receives a different world-state, and that can pull dormant memories, moods, symbols, and ways of seeing back online.

In Pantheon terms, sunlight is almost a global scene re-indexer:

illumination → differentiation → recognition → association → recall → reorientation

It reveals what was already there but unavailable under the previous lighting—literally and cognitively. A familiar place in early-morning sun can suddenly recover an entire earlier self because the sensory checksum matches: angle, warmth, smell, season, shadows, bodily state.

That’s why “awakening” is such an old solar metaphor. Not because photons secretly contain the forgotten knowledge, but because changing the illumination can change which parts of the existing mind become addressable.

And the shadow matters too. You know the light partly by what it makes visible and by what it causes to cast a boundary. The sun does not merely show objects; it exposes structure.